{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"web_search","id":"exec-0eb4347c-48b7-4f6f-90db-bb873a0e0391","query":"https://history.blueskyrangers.com/mattelelectronics/games/autoracing.html","action":{"type":"open_page","url":"https://history.blueskyrangers.com/mattelelectronics/games/autoracing.html"},"results":[{"type":"text_result","domain":"history.blueskyrangers.com","ref_id":"turn2view0","snippet":"Total lines: 271","title":"Auto Racing","url":"https://history.blueskyrangers.com/mattelelectronics/games/autoracing.html"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"history.blueskyrangers.com","ref_id":"turn2view1","snippet":"Total lines: 256","title":"Auto Racing instructions To the right of the Level Number is the OBJECT IN RIGHT HAND. To the left of the Object in Left Hand is the COMPASS, represented by a letter (N, W, S, E) that shows the direction you are facing (North, West, South, East). Around the Compass are the items currently in your PACK. There can be from none to four items in the Pack. To the right of the Object in Right Hand is the 3-WAY SCORE: three sets of numbers separated by slashes. The top set is your WAR/SPIRITUAL STRENGTH SCORE. The middle set is your ARMOR/RING DEFENSES SCORE. The bottom set is your WAR WEAPON/SPIRITUAL WEAPON SCORE. Above the 3-Way Score is your TREASURE SCORE (Castle Map only). YOUR COMMANDS * To LOOK at CASTLE MAP, press and hold either Upper Action Button. The FLASHING WHITE DOT will tell you where you are -- level and location -- in the maze system. Up to 12 levels can be seen on the map. The SMALL YELLOW CHEST in the center of the lowest level indicates the location of the TARMIN TREASURE...guarded by the MINOTAUR. This is the first of 256 levels where the treasure can be found. Each level down is filled with nastier monsters and magic items...and maybe the terrible Minotaur! You CANNOT look at the castle map while fighting a monster! * To MOVE FORWARD, TURN LEFT, or TURN RIGHT, press and hold DISC in corresponding position. * TO COUNT number of FOOD UNITS you have, press and hold Lower Left Action Button. You'll hear a series of CLICKS. Each click corresponds to a food unit. You can collect up to 99 units of food by picking up flour sacks of 6 to 9 units each. * To COUNT number of ARROWS you have, press and hold Lower Right Action Button. You'll hear a series of CLICKS. Each click corresponds to an arrow. You can collect up to 99 arrows by picking up quivers of 6 to 9 arrows each. * To pick up or drop objects, press PICK UP/DROP (1). This relates to your RIGHT HAND only. Your hand must be empty to pick up something, otherwise this command will swap what is in your hand with what is at your feet. * To swap objects from one hand to another, press SWAP HANDS (2). * To rotate objects in your pack, press ROTATE PACK (3). Up to 6 objects will rotate in a CLOCKWISE direction, one position at a time, occupying the following clock positions: 1, 3, 5, 7, 9 and 11 o'clock. * To swap an object in your pack with an object in your right hand, press SWAP PACK (6). You can only swap an object occupying the 3 o'clock position in your pack. * TO open an UNLOCKED CONTAINER at your feet, or a door or hidden door directly in front of you, press OPEN (4). Certain walls may contain a HIDDEN DOOR. If TRAPPED in a room, try this command when facing a wall. * To use an object in your right hand, or possibly act upon an object at your feet, press USE (5). * To attack a monster directly in front of you with the object in your right hand, press ATTACK (7). You cannot attack while the objects in your left and right hands are flashing (this means it's NOT your turn). Monsters often attack first. Wait for your turn. * To REGAIN both your WAR STRENGTH and SPIRITUAL STRENGTH after a battle, press REST (8). The amount of rest you'll get will depend on the number of food units you have picked up. Each unit of food can give you up to 5 units of war strength and 2 units of spiritual strength. * To GO DOWN a LEVEL into a new maze using the ladder at your feet, press USE LADDER (9). Once used, a ladder WON'T allow you to CLIMB UP a level again. However, when you reach level 256, the deepest one, a ladder will place you back up to level 1. * To see what is to your left, press GLANCE LEFT (CLEAR). * TO see what is to your right, press GLANCE RIGHT (ENTER). * To go back where you were last or retrace your last step, press RETREAT/RETRACE (0). Press once to retreat and the monster will take one LAST SHOT at you. If you continue pressing this command, the monster will continue hitting you until you let up on the Disc. GET READY Start by setting the skill level: Press 3 for Easiest Press 2 for Easy Press 1 for Medium Press DISC for Hard START YOUR JOURNEY! Once a skill level has been set, the game begins immediately with a brief display of the overall CASTLE MAP. The castle map comes and goes. You've been told where you are in the first maze level, by the FLASHING WHITE DOT. You've seen the ladders diagonally connecting the maze levels, and the TARMIN TREASURE (represented by a small YELLOW CHEST) right in the middle of the bottom level! Here are your INITIAL CONDITIONS, depending on your skill level chosen: EASIEST Minimum Maze Levels: 2 Possible Length of Game: 5 to 30 minutes Your Vulnerability: 1/4 Your War/Spiritual Strength: 18/9 Your Food: 9 Your Arrows: 9 EASY Minimum Maze Levels: 4 Possible Length of Game: 1/4 to 1 hour Your Vulnerability: 1/2 Your War/Spiritual Strength: 16/8 Your Food: 8 Your Arrows: 8 MEDIUM Minimum Maze Levels: 8 Possible Length of Game: 1/2 to 3 hours Your Vulnerability: 3/4 Your War/Spiritual Strength: 14/7 Your Food: 7 Your Arrows: 7 HARD Minimum Maze Levels: 12 Possible Length of Game: 1.5 to 5 hours Your Vulnerability: FULL Your War/Spiritual Strength: 12/6 Your Food: 6 Your Arrows: 6 Now you're on your own in the NW corner of the maze...heading EAST...inside an outer corridor. You carry a BOW in your right hand, some ARROWS in the quiver, a little FOOD in a sack, and an empty pack. Neither the quiver nor the sack are seen. The pack occupies the area on the screen where the COMPASS is, and is noticed only when stuffed with objects. Move in the direction you want to go, by pressing edge of DISC. Often, EYEBALL MURALS will appear to you. They are always in the outer corridors, MARKING the ENTRANCE to the interior of a maze. They come in three different colors. DARK GREEN indicates a war type maze, BLUE a spiritual type maze, and TAN a mixed type maze. KNOW THE MAZE Check your direction with the COMPASS. Look for OBJECTS and DOORS. Objects can be anything...such as weapons, containers, keys, useful items or treasures. Doors are usually easy to spot. But... If TRAPPED in a DUNGEON, a HALLWAY, or a SECTION of the MAZE which seems to repeat itself endlessly, look for possible HIDDEN DOORS. Confront any suspicious wall as you would confront a regular door and press OPEN (4). If a hidden door exists, it will open. If not, turn around...step by step...and try other walls...or other pathways. Beware of OTHER DOORS...endowed with weird powers... A LADDER in your way will provide a quick exit, DOWN into a DEEPER LEVEL. Approach the ladder and press USE LADDER (9). A suction sound will immediately transfer you! You may want to check your new position on the castle map, if you're still displayed on it. Sometimes you will encounter GATES in hallways. Going through a gate moves you over to an adjacent maze, but it may JUMBLE your WAR/SPIRITUAL strength SCORE. And you CANNOT go back through it! (REST AFTERWARD to regain some points and check your new score.) Gates come in 3 colors: GREEN (makes you more war-like), BLUE (makes you more spiritual), and TAN (won't change you at all). TREASURES DISAPPEAR when picked up, because you either carry them in your pockets or wear them. THEIR VALUES are recorded only on the castle map, in BLACK, directly above the 3-way scoring display. QUIVERS and SACKS of FLOUR also DISAPPEAR when picked up. You'll be carrying them on your back! Other objects, like USEFUL ITEMS, may DISAPPEAR when picked up or used with your right hand. Or they may make you disappear...making you INVISIBLE to all monsters but the Minotaur (while also making the monsters invisible to you!) Such objects have SPIRITUAL POWERS. To use a spiritual object, and it must be in your right hand or at your feet, press USE (5). Use immediately -- rather than keeping it around -- to build up your spiritual power, increase your score, and stand better chances of SURVIVING ATTACKING MONSTERS. Check spiritual points, if any, right of the slash of any of the three displayed scores. ATTACKING! You must have a weapon or magical item for use in your RIGHT HAND to fight. If you don't, swap a weapon from your left hand or pack. Or pick up a weapon that you find. Try to be prepared with a shield in your left hand. If order of objects in pack needs rotating, do so, and get your most effective weapon out! Remember, many weapons and items will VANISH upon their use in battle. So keep several weapons in your pack to ensure victory. Watch both YOUR SCORE and the MONSTER'S SCORE before and after every round of attack. Work out a BATTLE STRATEGY. Some monsters will knock you out with just one or two blows...if you're not careful! Avoid attacking if your score looks weaker than the monster's. Press RETREAT/RETRACE and move away. Chances are you can PICK UP MORE WEAPONS in the hallways and dungeons and ATTACK LATER! You can only attack a monster STANDING DIRECTLY IN FRONT of you, although a monster may attack you from a nearby position. Sometimes he might just run smack in front of you from BEHIND a DOOR that behaves strangely because of an OMINOUS SPELL. Watch out for those SURPRISE ATTACKS! After the initial assault, you ALTERNATE ATTACKS. If you attack first , you CANNOT attack again until the monster has thrown something at you. The exchange will be brief if either you or the monster is much stronger. Or it may last quite a while if there is a balance of forces. The BATTLE ENDS when either you or the monster DIES...or when you RETREAT. Monsters NEVER retreat. A dying monster will disappear in a cloud of smoke. His score will vanish too. If you DIE, your score will end up with a 0 on either side of the slash of your WAR/SPIRITUAL STRENGTH scoring display. However, cheer up! If you are worthy, you'll be REINCARNATED! And that means, a new opportunity for you to win! SCORING Your 3-WAY SCORING DISPLAY on BOTH MAPS plus your SPECIAL TREASURE VALUE SCORING DISPLAY on the CASTLE MAP tell you how you're doing. Anything LEFT of a slash on the 3-way scoring is a WAR-TYPE score, anything RIGHT of a slash is a SPIRITUAL score. At the BOTTOM is the score of the object (WAR WEAPON or SPIRITUAL WEAPON) you have picked up for use in COMBAT...or BUILD YOUR STRENGTH in preparation for such a combat. The score will remain on the screen as long as you hold the object in your RIGHT HAND. In the MIDDLE is your ARMOR/RING body DEFENSES score. At the TOP is your overall STRENGTH (WAR and SPIRITUAL) score. Keep it high! A similar overall STRENGTH score is kept for the monster attacking you! His comes in green or blue colors. GREEN for WAR-derived STRENGTH and BLUE for SPIRITUAL-derived STRENGTH. This score will appear DURING a BATTLE only and on the spot on the screen where normally the maze level indication appears. The score that appears initially is the weakest of his two -- WAR or SPIRITUAL. Thereafter, is the score that was last damaged by your weapon used. Notice that your MAXIMUM STRENGTH score (top line) under normal conditions is 49/24. But using useful items such as war or spiritual books or certain potions may change that! Notice also that your MAXIMUM WEAPON score (bottom line) is 99/0 (if WAR type) or 0/65 (if SPIRITUAL). Always check the SECRETS OF THE UNIVERSE tables to plan your STRATEGIES...and SURVIVE! And ultimately WIN the GAME! [NOTE: In the original hand-written instructions by programmer Tom Loughry, Tom suggests that the true gamer will have more fun by NOT reading the SECRETS OF THE UNIVERSE section, instead learning the secrets for himself while playing.] SECRETS OF THE UNIVERSE WAR WEAPON TYPES BOWS: Use 1 arrow at a time; bows may break KNIVES: Vanish when used in an attack AXES: Vanish when used in an attack DARTS: Vanish when used in an attack SPEARS: Vanish when used in an attack CROSSBOWS: Use 1 arrow at a time; crossbows may break WAR WEAPON COLORS & POWERS TAN (wood/leather): Regular Power ORANGE (rusty iron): Greater Power BLUE (steel): Fair Power GRAY (silver): Medium Power YELLOW (gold): High Power WHITE (platinum): Super Power ARMOR TYPES SMALL SHIELDS: Hold in left hand during fight LARGE SHIELDS: Hold in left hand during fight GAUNTLETS: Use to put on; best color is kept HAUBERKS: Use to put on; best color is kept HELMETS: Use to put on; best color is kept BREASTPLATES: Use to put on; best color is kept * Wearing a piece of armor will protect you a little from war weapons thrown at you by monsters. ARMOR COLORS & POWERS: Same as WAR WEAPONS SPIRITUAL WEAPON TYPES SCROLLS: Reusable; may break SMALL FIREBALLS: Vanish when used in attack SMALL LIGHTNING BOLTS: Vanish when used in attack LARGE FIREBALLS: Vanish when used in attack LARGE LIGHTNING BOLTS: Vanish when used in attack BOOKS: Reusable; may break SPIRITUAL WEAPON COLORS & POWERS BLUE: Regular Power GRAY: Greater Power WHITE: Fair Power PINK: Medium Power RED: High Power PURPLE: Super Power RING TYPES SMALL RING: Use to put on; best color is kept LARGE RING: Use to put on; best color is kept * Wearing a ring will protect you a little from spiritual weapons thrown at you by monsters. RING COLORS & POWER: Same as SPIRITUAL WEAPONS CONTAINER TYPES MONEY BELTS: Contents REGULAR/Open to grab contents SMALL BAGS: Contents BETTER/Open to grab contents BOXES: Contents FAIR/Locked; use key to open and grab contents PACKS: Contents MEDIUM/Locked; use key to open and grab contents LARGE BAGS: Contents GREAT/Open to grab contents CHESTS: Contents SUPER/Locked; use key to open and grab contents * Containers may contain bombs, which will hurt you when they blow up. There are 3 types of bombs. Nastier bombs will be found in better containers. There is no way of avoiding their blast. * When opened or unlocked, containers reveal a treasure or a useful item. The value of the treasure or item found depends on the type and quality of the container. Better treasures are found in better containers. * Use of a small potion increases the potential of all containers significantly while in effect. Attacking a monster with a container will make him take the container and forget about the fight. CONTAINER COLORS & POWERS TAN: Mild Quality ORANGE: Good Quality BLUE: Best Quality TREASURE TYPES COINS: 10 (silver), 30 (gold), 170 (platinum) NECKLACES: 20 (silver), 70 (gold), 200 (platinum) INGOTS: 50 (silver), 350 (gold), 450 (platinum) LAMPS: 100 (silver), 150 (gold), 220 (platinum) CHALICES: 120 (silver), 250 (gold), 400 (platinum) CROWNS: 300 (silver), 500 (gold), 600 (platinum) * Picking up treasures with your right hand automatically scores them for you. Read their special score in black on your overall castle map only. Watch each treasure being picked up disappear, since you'll be carrying it in your pocket or wearing it. USEFUL ITEMS TYPES KEYS TAN: Unlock tan containers ORANGE: Unlock tan or orange containers BLUE: Unlock any container WAR BOOKS (VANISH ON USE) BLUE: Increase your experience with war weapons; turn your war strength score blue when in use; raise maximum score to 99 PINK: Increase your experience with war weapons; turn your war strength score tan when in use; raise maximum score to 149 PURPLE: Increase your experience with war weapons; turn your war strength score white when in use; raise maximum score to 199 SPIRITUAL BOOKS (VANISH ON USE) BLUE: Increase your experience with spiritual weapons; turn your war strength score blue when in use; raise maximum score to 49 PINK: Increase your experience with spiritual weapons; turn your war strength score tan when in use; raise maximum score to 74 PURPLE: Increase your experience with spiritual weapons; turn your war strength score white when in use; raise maximum score to 99 SMALL POTIONS (VANISH ON USE) BLUE: Refresh war & spiritual strength to maximum; help in fights, but lose 1 turn during fights PINK: Help find better things in containers; magic ends with a little \"whoosh\" noise PURPLE: Make you invisible to all demons except the Minotaur (while making them invisible to you); magic ends with a little \"whoosh\" noise LARGE POTIONS (VANISH ON USE) BLUE: Raise war strength score by 10; help in fights, but lose 1 turn during fights PINK: Raise spiritual power score by 10; help you, but lose 1 turn PURPLE: Switch war & spiritual strength traits; help in fights, but lose 1 turn during fights SPECIAL BOOKS (NEVER VANISH) BLUE: Teleport books move you forward in maze rooms in spite of walls, but not into monsters PINK: Vision books allow you to see through walls for a time PURPLE: Midas books turn treasures & war weapons platinum at your feet * Useful items are found only in containers. BAD MONSTERS GIANT ANTS: Blue, Pink, Purple DWARFS: Yellow, Tan, Orange GIANT SCORPIONS: Blue, Pink, Purple DWARFS WITH SHIELDS: Yellow, Tan, Orange GIANT SNAKES: Blue, Pink, Purple ALLIGATORS: Blue, Pink, Purple DRAGONS: Blue, Pink, Purple * Bad monsters use spiritual weapons only. * Bad monsters are listed in order of increasing badness NASTY MONSTERS SKELETONS WITH SHIELDS: White, Gray, Orange SKELETONS WITHOUT SHIELDS: White, Gray, Orange CLOAKED SKELETONS WITH SHIELDS: White, Gray, Orange CLOAKED SKELETONS WITHOUT SHIELDS: White, Gray, Orange GIANTS WITH SHIELDS: Yellow, Tan, Orange GIANTS WITHOUT SHIELDS: Yellow, Tan, Orange * Nasty monsters use war weapons only. * Nasty monsters are listed in order of increasing nastiness. HORRIBLE MONSTERS GHOULS (SHORT HOODED & ROBED) WITH SHIELDS: White, Gray, Orange WITHOUT SHIELDS: White, Gray, Orange WRAITHS (TALL, HOODED & ROBED) WITH SHIELDS: White, Gray, Orange WITHOUT SHIELDS: White, Gray, Orange MINOTAUR: Purple (Guards the Tarmin treasure) * Horrible monsters use either spiritual or war weapons. * In harder two skill levels, horrible monsters will shatter your weakest of two scores -- war strength or spiritual strength. * All together there are 52 types of monsters; you'll find more of the nasty and horrible ones in the deepest levels...down to level 256...if you care to find them! * To win the game you must slay the Minotaur and grab the Tarmin treasure! -------------- (c)1983, 1998 Intellivision Productions Inc.","url":"https://history.blueskyrangers.com/instructions/autoracinginst.html"},{"type":"text_result","ref_id":"turn2view2","title":"Internal Error"},{"type":"text_result","ref_id":"turn2view3","title":"Internal Error"}]}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'ls vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff && cat vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/README.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'ls vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff && cat vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/README.md'","aggregated_output":"AI_GUIDE.md\nAPI-INDEX.md\nAPI.md\nREADME.md\ndocs\ngame\nindex.html\nkits\nmodules\nmy-3d2dge-agent.js\nmy-3d2dge.js\npackage.json\nsrc\ntools\n# my-3D2dge handoff package (v0.89.1)\n\nEverything a coding agent needs to build, run, test and check a game on the full my-3D2dge engine, in one folder of text\nfiles. You don't need the engine's repository, and you don't need to read the engine's source below its header.\n\n**The engine is yours to change.** This copy is yours to fix, extend or rewrite in any way you see fit: `my-3d2dge.js`\nis the engine, readable, with every module, and every page and test here loads it (`modules/` and `my-3d2dge-agent.js`\nare separate copies). If you hand the game back, a line on what you changed and why helps: it shows what the engine\nlacks.\n\n## Start here\n\n1. **Read `API.md`**, the API card: a complete game to start from, what x, y and z mean in each view, the rules, the\n   common mistakes and the API by area. It is the one page to read whole.\n2. **Start from a game, not an empty file.** The kit for your genre (`kits/`): the card's Genre playbook names one for\n   each genre, and `kits/README.md` says which file is which. `kits/<id>.js` is a complete game in that genre, readable\n   (its title, play and end, the modules it rests on, sound, touch controls), `kits/<id>.html` plays it, and\n   `node kits/test.mjs <id>` runs it headless. Or the template, `game/`: a small complete game (climbing, a guard with\n   sight, an HTML HUD, scenes) that `game/index.html` plays and `game/test.mjs` tests without a browser. Copy one into\n   `game/` and make it yours.\n3. **Search `API-INDEX.md`** when you need a name: every public name in the engine, one line each, with its signature,\n   units and defaults, and the height rules at the top. `grep -n \"ledge\\|landing\" API-INDEX.md`. Don't read it whole.\n4. **`AI_GUIDE.md`** has the recipes: genre by genre, the look to aim for, the frame order, heights, recorded sessions.\n5. **Don't build a pause menu, settings, key rebinding, full screen or a loading screen**: every game has them (`API.md`,\n   \"Standard features\"). Adjust them with `new E.Game({ menu: { items, settings, controls, quit } })`; heavy setup goes in\n   `game.start({ boot: { steps } })`.\n6. **Objects without drawing code:** read `docs/ASSETS.md` whole before you draw a barrel, a table or a tree.\n   `r.shape('barrel', x, y, 0, { facing })` draws one of the core library's in every view; `node tools/asset-find.mjs\n   \"market stall\"` searches it and the store (`src/assets/sets/`: about a thousand objects, materials and sprites,\n   each with where it came from), and `node tools/asset-sheet.mjs mine.js#stool` draws one you wrote, to look at\n   before you use it.\n7. **A game in real 3D** (first person, a light gun, a racer on banked roads, flight, a dungeon crawled in 3D, fixed\n   cameras): the 2D views have no perspective camera, but the engine has a 3D add-on, `World3D` (three.js r182 and\n   Rapier 0.19.3 under the 2D engine, which keeps the HUD, menus and sound). It is not in this folder: take\n   `engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js` (its header is its manual), `vendor/` and the 3D kit closest to your game\n   (`src/starter3d/`) from the repository, github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge, and see them play at\n   my-3d2dge.vercel.app/3d.\n\n## What is here\n\n| File | What it is |\n| --- | --- |\n| `my-3d2dge.js` | the full engine, readable (one file, no dependencies): the core, then every module |\n| `modules/` | each engine module on its own, when there are any (one general system; its header is its manual), to load after the agent edition |\n| `my-3d2dge-agent.js` | the agent edition: the essential engine, whose header is a shorter manual (a game written for it runs on the full engine) |\n| `API.md`, `API-INDEX.md`, `AI_GUIDE.md` | the card, the index, the guide |\n| `kits/` | the 2D genre kits, readable: each kit's game (`<id>.js`) and the page that plays it (`<id>.html`), the table of them (`README.md`) and their headless test (`test.mjs`) |\n| `game/` | the template game: `index.html`, `game.js`, `test.mjs` |\n| `tools/check.mjs` | plays a game in headless Chromium, switches its views, and reports errors, what is on screen, and `game.debug.snapshot()` |\n| `tools/filmstrip.mjs` | records frames into one picture, to judge motion (`--seed 1` makes it repeatable, `--compare` marks what changed) |\n| `tools/replay.mjs` | replays a recorded play session exactly (`E.session`, a bug seen on a phone) |\n| `docs/ASSETS.md` | the asset card: still objects and materials as data (`r.shape`, `map.solid`, `E.MATERIALS`), the core library, writing one |\n| `tools/asset-find.mjs`, `asset-sheet.mjs`, `asset-set.mjs` | search the core library and the store; draw an object in every view and lint it; cut the few entries a game uses into a set of its own (`asset-lib.mjs` is theirs) |\n| `src/assets/` | the asset store: sets of objects and materials converted from CC0 libraries (Kenney, KayKit, ambientCG), with their catalogs. A game loads one with its script and `E.addAssets(window.ASSETS['kenney-furniture'])`; the Asset Lab shows them all at my-3d2dge.vercel.app/asset-lab |\n| `package.json` | pins Playwright for the tools |\n\nNot here: the 3D engine (three.js and Rapier) and its kits, the playbook's 3D rows. They are on the site\n(my-3d2dge.vercel.app/3d) and in the repository (github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge); here a 3D game is built on the 2D\nengine's views (`kits/README.md`, 3D).\n\n## Run, test, check\n\n```sh\nnpm install                          # Playwright, for the tools (once)\nnpx playwright install chromium      # its browser (once)\nnode game/test.mjs                   # the game's headless test: no browser, a second\nnode tools/check.mjs game/index.html # play it in a browser: errors, screenshots, its snapshot\nnode kits/test.mjs arcade            # a kit, headless: it plays, and its proof gives the same hash twice\nnode tools/check.mjs kits/arcade.html#arcade/maze   # a kit in a browser, at a part (its deep link)\nnode tools/filmstrip.mjs game/index.html --steps \"wait:800 press:Enter wait:500 rec:16:2 down:KeyD wait:600\" --seed 1\n```\n\nOpen `game/index.html` in a browser to play (or serve the folder: `npx serve`). To hand a single file to a person, inline\nthe two scripts into the page, writing every `</script` inside them as `<\\/script` (the agent edition's header has one,\nand the page would end there).\n\n## Testing a game without a browser\n\n`new E.Game()` is headless in Node (or with `{ headless: true }`): no screen, sound or DOM, and `draw` is skipped unless\nthe game is made with `draw: true` (the template is): then each `advance` draws a frame on a stand-in, and a draw error\nlands in `game.errors`, as it would on a player's screen.\n\n- `game.advance(seconds)` runs exact fixed steps of 1/120 s at once: `advance(1)` is 120 updates.\n- `game.input.tap('jump')` presses an action for one step, `hold('right')` holds one down, `hold('right', false)` lets go.\n- `game.debug = { snapshot() { return {...} }, teleport(x, y, z) {...} }` is your game's handle: tests read it, and\n  `tools/check.mjs` and `tools/replay.mjs` print it.\n- `game.stop()`, `game.start()` and `game.dispose()` stop, resume and end a game.\n- `game.go('play')` fades for `game.fadeTime` (.22 s) before the new scene enters: advance past it, or set\n  `game.fadeTime = 0` in a test.\n- A scene change clears `game.after` and `game.every` timers (`{ keep: true }` outlives it); those made before the first\n  scene live into it. It ends a hit-stop; `game.timeScale` stays (restore a slow motion with a kept timer). A boot step's `game.go(name)` enters that scene when the boot ends.\n- Draw changes nothing, and nothing your update or input relies on (a click target, what is on screen) may be made only\n  in `draw`: `E.Menu`'s rows come from its update (`menu.rowAt(i)` is entry i's box, for a test's click), and the camera\n  (`game.cam`) says where the screen is in update.\n- `E.store` keeps values for the visit in Node, which has no `localStorage` (`E.store.clear()` between games in one test).\n- A script's `tap` and `hold` are no device: `input.label()` keeps showing the keys or buttons the player used last.\n\nThe same inputs give the same game on every machine and at every refresh rate: the clock is an exact fixed step.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat vendor/hub-repo/lib/hub/README.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat vendor/hub-repo/lib/hub/README.md'","aggregated_output":"# AI Hub Session Protocol v1\n\nAn **AI Hub session** is up to four players, each on their own device,\nplaying rounds of games one after another. The hub runs everything around a\nround: the invite, the lobby, the seats and names, the network, the clock, the\nscoreboard and the results. This page defines how a game takes part in a\nround, and how the copies of a game send data to each other.\n\n**https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md**\n\nThe hub tests every game when it joins the library, with the same test\ndescribed in *Test it*, and keeps a game that fails it out of sessions.\n\n- Client: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js\n- Harness: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\n\nTo ask an agent for it, add this to the game's prompt:\n\n> This game must support AI Hub sessions. Follow\n> https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md\n> (AI Hub Session Protocol v1) and pass the harness's automatic check.\n\n---\n\n## Modes\n\nA game declares one mode.\n\n| Mode | What each copy runs | What the copies send each other |\n|---|---|---|\n| `parallel` | its own copy of the round | nothing |\n| `ghost` | its own copy of the round | each copy's own player: where it is and what it is doing |\n| `shared-world` | one world for all players, run by the host's copy | what each player does, to the host; the world, from the host |\n\n- **Parallel**: every player plays their own copy of the round, from the same\n  start. Each copy reports its score to the hub.\n- **Ghost**: as parallel, and each copy also shows the other players'\n  characters where they are, when they are in the same area of the game.\n  Those characters do not affect anything in the copy that shows them.\n- **Shared world**: all players play in one world. The host's copy runs that\n  world; the other copies send it what their own player does, and show the\n  world the host sends them.\n\nGames tagged with the older modes keep working: `race` is played as ghost,\n`co-op` and `versus` as shared world.\n\n---\n\n## A round\n\n1. The hub loads the game on every player's device, and the players get 10\n   to 30 seconds with the game's card, longer if one asks for more time: what\n   the game is, how to win, the controls. The card is the hub's, from the\n   game's tags, or the game's own (*The card*).\n2. The game connects and calls `ready()`.\n3. The round starts on the hub's clock, on every device at once.\n4. The game reports how it goes.\n5. The round ends when every player has a result, when the host of a shared\n   world finishes it, or when the hub calls time. The hub shows the results\n   over the game, then removes it.\n\n### Tags\n\nIn the game's `<head>`:\n\n```html\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"MIN-MAX\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"MODE\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"GOAL\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"CONTROLS\">\n```\n\n- `hub:session-players`: the smallest and largest number of players the game\n  takes in a session. The hub deals the game only when the number of players\n  is in that range.\n- `hub:session-mode`: `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world`.\n- `hub:session-goal` and `hub:session-howto`: how to win and the game's\n  controls, shown on the hub's card.\n\nOptional:\n\n- `hub:session-minutes`: how many minutes a round of the game takes. The hub\n  shows it and uses it to decide when to call time.\n- `hub:webgpu` with `required`: the game cannot run without WebGPU. The hub\n  then deals it only when every player's browser has WebGPU.\n\n### The client\n\n[session.js](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js)\nis pasted into the game unmodified, as the first script in `<head>`, right\nafter `<meta charset>`. In a session it:\n\n- seeds `Math.random`, in parallel and ghost, before any of the game's own\n  scripts run, so it gives the same numbers on every copy;\n- gives the game an empty `localStorage` and `sessionStorage` of its own, so\n  nothing stored on the device carries into the round, and nothing the round\n  stores reaches the device's own storage;\n- hands `requestFullscreen()` and `exitFullscreen()` to the hub, which takes\n  its whole page fullscreen; `document.fullscreenElement` and\n  `fullscreenchange` follow the hub's page.\n\nThe hub replaces an unmodified copy of the client with each new version. An\nedited copy is not replaced.\n\n### Joining\n\n- `AIHub.active` is `true` from the first line when the hub opened the page.\n- In a session, the game opens straight into the round: no title screen,\n  menus or prompts before it, other than its own card (*The card*).\n- `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats: [min, max] })` returns a promise of the\n  round's `info`, or of `null` when there is no hub after all; with `null`\n  the game runs as it does on its own.\n- `AIHub.ready()` is called once, when the game has loaded and the round can\n  start, with no key press: the players answer the card.\n- The round starts when the card's countdown ends or every player pressed\n  Ready, and every game has called `ready()`. A game still loading then gets\n  20 more seconds; after that its player sits the round out.\n\n`info` (also on `AIHub` after `connect()`):\n\n| Field | |\n|---|---|\n| `me` | this copy's player id |\n| `players` | `[{ id, name, color }]`, ids `0..N-1` in seat order |\n| `host`, `isHost` | the id of the copy that runs a shared world, and whether it is this one |\n| `server` | `true` only on a copy that hosts a shared world with no player of its own |\n| `mode`, `round`, `limitMs` | the mode, the round number, and the round's hard limit after the start |\n| `seed` | a number, the same on every copy, behind `AIHub.rng(label)` |\n\nNames come from the hub and are never inserted into the page as HTML. Colours\nfollow the seat: `#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`.\n\n### The card\n\nBefore every round the players get 10 to 30 seconds to learn what comes\nnext and say when they are ready. A player can ask for more time, which adds\nten seconds; while more than half of them are asking, the countdown holds.\nBy default the hub shows its own card over the game. A game can make the\ncard its own, as part of the game:\n\n```js\nAIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card: true });\n```\n\nThe hub's card then steps aside when the game calls `ready()`, and the game's\nscreen is the card until `start`. How it looks and sounds is the game's: its\ntitle, its music, its world, the players lining up. Introduce the game the way\nit deserves.\n\nWhile it is on, the card shows:\n\n- the game's name;\n- the players, each in their colour, and which of them are ready;\n- how to win the round;\n- the controls this player will use;\n- the time left, from the `card` event.\n\nAnd it takes two answers, from the keyboard, a controller, or a tap or click\non the card:\n\n- **Ready**: Enter, Cross (button 0), or a tap on Ready:\n  `AIHub.answer('ready')`.\n- **More time**: Triangle (button 3), a key the card names, or a tap on More\n  time: `AIHub.answer('more')`. Answering `more` again takes it back.\n\nNothing done on the card changes the round. It has no menus, settings or\nchoices, and play begins only at `start`. In parallel and ghost it draws no\nnumbers from `Math.random`, which would change the round's start: use\n`AIHub.rng('card')`.\n\n`card` arrives whenever it changes, and every second while the countdown runs:\n\n| Field | |\n|---|---|\n| `left` | milliseconds until the round starts by itself; `null` while paused or starting |\n| `paused` | `true` while most players asked for more time |\n| `starting` | `true` once the countdown is over or everyone is ready: `start` follows when every game has loaded |\n| `ready` | the ids of the players who are ready |\n| `asking` | how many players asked for more time (never who) |\n| `asked` | `true` when this player asked for more time |\n\n### The start\n\n`start` arrives when the round starts. Play begins three seconds later, when\n`AIHub.time()` reaches 0. `AIHub.time()` is the same timeline on every device,\ngive or take about 15 ms: negative during the countdown, `-Infinity` before\n`start`.\n\n### Reporting\n\n- `AIHub.progress({ score, label })`, in parallel and ghost: the player's\n  standing during the round. `score` is a number, higher is ahead; `label` is\n  text for the hub's scoreboard. The hub takes the latest about once a second.\n- `AIHub.result({ time })` or `AIHub.result({ score })`, in parallel and\n  ghost, once, when the player's round is over. `time` is milliseconds of\n  `AIHub.time()`, lower wins; `score` is a number, higher wins.\n- `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, won })`, in a shared world, called once,\n  by the host only:\n  - `standings`: arrays of player ids, best first; players in the same array\n    tie. It lists every player still in the round once; anyone left out is\n    placed last.\n  - `scores` (optional): `{ id: number }`, shown on the scoreboard.\n  - `won` (optional): `true` or `false` when the round is a team result.\n\n### Time-up and the end\n\n- The hub may call time while the round is still going. On `time-up`, the\n  game reports within `graceMs`: a `result()` in parallel and ghost, a\n  `finish()` from the host in a shared world. A shared world with no\n  `finish()` by then is void.\n- `end` is the last message of a round. After it, the game takes no input and\n  sends nothing, and its sound stops. The hub shows its results over the\n  game, then removes it.\n\n| `end` reason | |\n|---|---|\n| `finished` | the round counted; `standings` is the final order |\n| `void` | it didn't count (no result, a fatal error, skipped) |\n| `host-left` | the host of a shared world left |\n| `sat-out` | this player missed the ready deadline |\n| `session-ended` | the session closed |\n\n### Leaving\n\n- `leave` arrives with `{ id, reason }` when a player leaves: `left`,\n  `disconnected`, `not-ready` or `kicked`. It is final for the round.\n- A parallel or ghost round goes on whoever leaves, the host included.\n- A shared-world round is void when its host leaves, isn't ready, or has its\n  tab hidden for more than 20 seconds.\n- `AIHub.fail(message)`: the game cannot run in this browser. From the host\n  of a shared world it voids the round; from any other copy it counts as\n  that player leaving.\n\n### Scoring\n\n- Parallel and ghost: players with a `time` rank first, lowest first; then\n  the rest by `score`. A player with no result is ranked by their last\n  progress.\n- Shared world: the host's `standings`. With `won`, the results show a team\n  result instead of medals.\n- Each player gets one point for every player they beat.\n\n`result()` and `finish()` may also carry `stats` and `awards`, shown with the\nresults:\n\n- in `result()`: `stats` is `{ name: value }` and `awards` is\n  `[{ title, detail }]`, for this player;\n- in `finish()`: `stats` is `{ id: { name: value } }` and `awards` is\n  `[{ id, title, detail }]`, for each player.\n\nPlaces and points come only from the standings, `time` and `score`.\n\n---\n\n## Sending data between copies\n\n### Parallel\n\nThe copies send nothing to each other. `send()` is not used.\n\n### Ghost\n\nEach copy sends its own player's position and what the player is doing to\nthe other copies with `send()`. Each copy shows the other players' characters\nonly while they are in the same area of the game as its own player, and those\ncharacters do not affect its world. `Math.random` is seeded as in parallel.\n\n### Shared world\n\n- The host's copy (`AIHub.isHost`) runs the world. The other copies send the\n  host what their own player does, and show the world the host sends them.\n- The hub can run the host's copy with no player of its own: `AIHub.server`\n  is `true` and `AIHub.me` is not among `AIHub.players`. The host's copy runs\n  the world in that case too, with no local player, camera or input.\n- `Math.random` is not seeded. `AIHub.rng(label)` gives the same numbers on\n  every copy.\n\n### `send()` and `message`\n\n- `AIHub.send(data, { to, reliable })` sends `data` to every other copy, or\n  to the one player `to`. It returns `false` when the message is dropped.\n- `AIHub.on('message', (data, from) => …)` receives it; `from` is the\n  sender's player id. A copy never receives its own messages. Messages sent\n  before another copy has loaded are queued for it.\n- `data` is JSON, at most 16 KB.\n- At most 20 messages a second leave each copy, with bursts up to 40; the\n  client drops the rest.\n- Reliable messages (the default) arrive in the order they were sent.\n  Messages sent with `reliable: false` may be lost or arrive out of order. An\n  unreliable message larger than about 1,200 bytes travels as several packets\n  and is lost whole if any of them is.\n\n---\n\n## Rules\n\nIn a session:\n\n- **Network only through the hub.** No WebSocket, WebRTC, or `fetch` to a\n  game server or public broker. Loading the game's own files is fine.\n- **No communication between players.** No chat, voice, emotes or custom\n  names. Names come from the hub.\n- **The page stays.** No `alert`, `confirm` or `prompt`; no `window.open`,\n  new tabs or navigating away. Quitting, restarting or returning to a title\n  screen does nothing; the hub starts and ends rounds.\n- **Create + Options is the hub's.** On a controller, holding the two\n  together opens the hub's corner bar; the game ignores Options while Create\n  is held.\n\nEverything on this page applies only in a session (`AIHub.active`). Outside\none, the game is not affected.\n\n---\n\n## Test it\n\nThe harness plays the hub: it loads the game 1–4 times on a simulated network\nand runs the checks the hub runs when a game joins the library. Serve it next\nto the game:\n\n```bash\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/bridge.js\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/round.js\npython3 -m http.server 8000\n```\n\nThen open `http://localhost:8000/harness.html?game=GAME.html&players=2&auto=1`.\nThe hub's own test also runs ghost and shared-world games with\n`&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`, again with\n`&scenario=host-leave`, and shared worlds once more with\n`&players=2&scenario=server`.\n\nThe checklist fills in as it runs. **A game passes when\n`window.__hubCheck.pass` is true**; `document.body.dataset.complete` becomes\n`\"true\"` at the end. It checks:\n\n- the tags, `connect()`, `ready()` with no key press, and the start;\n- with the game's own card, that Enter answers Ready;\n- parallel and ghost: `Math.random` the same on every copy, and progress from\n  every copy; parallel sends nothing, ghost sends;\n- shared world: traffic both ways, and a round hosted by a copy with no\n  player of its own;\n- a player leaving, the host leaving, time-up, a valid result, and every copy\n  told the round ended;\n- no protocol problems, errors, dialogs, popups or sockets.\n\n**New round**, **Time up**, **Remove player** and **Hide host tab** run the\ncases by hand; clicking a player's frame gives it the keyboard and mouse.\n`&limit=` sets the round's length in seconds and `&seed=` fixes the seed. The\ngame is served from the harness's own origin.\n\nIn the AI Hub repository, `node scripts/hub-check.mjs <game>` runs the same\nchecks with Playwright, then opens the game outside a session to see it still\nruns there.\n\n---\n\n## Reference\n\n### Tags\n\n| Tag | |\n|---|---|\n| `hub:session` | `1`: this protocol |\n| `hub:session-players` | `MIN-MAX`: the number of players the game takes in a session |\n| `hub:session-mode` | `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world` |\n| `hub:session-goal` | how to win, shown on the hub's card |\n| `hub:session-howto` | the controls, shown on the hub's card |\n| `hub:session-minutes` | optional: how many minutes a round takes |\n| `hub:webgpu` | optional: `required` when the game cannot run without WebGPU |\n\n### API\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| `AIHub.active` | `true` in a session, from the first line |\n| `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card? })` | → `Promise<info \\| null>`; `null`: no hub. `card: true`: the game shows its own card |\n| `AIHub.ready()` | once, when the round can start |\n| `AIHub.answer('ready' \\| 'more')` | on the game's own card, before `start` |\n| `AIHub.time()` | milliseconds since play began: negative in the countdown, `-Infinity` before `start` |\n| `AIHub.on(event, fn)` | returns a function that removes the handler |\n| `AIHub.progress({ score, label })` | parallel and ghost |\n| `AIHub.result({ time \\| score, stats?, awards? })` | parallel and ghost, once |\n| `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores?, won?, stats?, awards? })` | shared world, the host, once |\n| `AIHub.send(data, { to, reliable })` | ghost and shared world |\n| `AIHub.rng(label)` | a generator of numbers in `[0, 1)`, the same on every copy, one stream per label |\n| `AIHub.seeded` | `true` when the client seeded `Math.random` |\n| `AIHub.fail(message)` | the game cannot run in this browser |\n\n`AIHub.probe()` and `AIHub.inspect()` exist for older games; the hub does not\nread them.\n\n| Event | Arguments |\n|---|---|\n| `card` | `{ left, paused, starting, ready, asking, asked }`, with the game's own card |\n| `start` | `{ in }`: milliseconds until play begins |\n| `message` | `data, from` |\n| `leave` | `{ id, reason }` |\n| `time-up` | `{ graceMs }` |\n| `end` | `{ reason, standings }` |\n\nEvents that arrive before a handler is attached are kept, and delivered in\norder when the first handler is attached. Fields and events a newer hub adds\nreach the game through the client unchanged.\n\n### Versions\n\nThis is protocol **1**, client **1.4.0**. Changes within version 1 only add:\ngames on older clients keep working. A change that would break a game becomes\nprotocol 2, with its own `hub:session` value.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
