{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_53","type":"agent_message","text":"The tour flow, car/course selection, settings, dev sliders, and local seat assignment are implemented. The direct connection flow now creates real invitation/reply descriptions, and Hub play uses a host authority loop that can run without a local player. Next I’ll test the bundled HTML in the browser for input and integration failures.\n"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_54","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json,pathlib\ninfo={\n'date':'2026-11-06 04:12:47','source':'Auto Racing (Mattel Intellivision, 1980; APh / Larry Zwick)','plan':'PLAN.md','genre':'Arcade circuit racing and pursuit competition','perspective':'Stable north-up overhead; native 80-degree top-down view','tech':'JavaScript, HTML/CSS, my-3D2dge 0.89.1 (8ecc2f6), AI Hub protocol 1 / session client 1.4.0 (35a1047), WebRTC data channels; Node 24 build tools','artDirection':'Original shaded code-drawn retro pixel countryside; native shapes, materials, roads, cars and depth-sorted props','animation':'Native engine procedural shape groups, tweens and particles; steered wheels, suspension, windmill sails, skids, dust, impacts and water splashes','audio':'Web Audio synthesized engine, slip, surfaces, cues and gibberish crew; original procedural title/driving/finish music, initial music gain 0.10; no audio files','online':'Shared-world host-authoritative AI Hub duels; standalone manual offer/answer WebRTC duels with matched cars; couch two-player and AI duels. Cross-NAT connectivity unknown.','testing':'Unlimited headless/state testing; one quiet test command; installed Playwright/browser stand-ins; screenshots for composition only; no FPS target','research':'Original Mattel instructions and corrected Blue Sky Rangers history, scanned manual illustrations, archival gameplay still, pinned engine/Hub contracts, official WebRTC guidance. Original music and exact course dimensions unknown.','environment':'Codex CLI (headless); Ubuntu Linux on WSL2','model':'OpenAI, gpt-6.1-sol, reasoning effort max','usage':'added on import'}\nr={'sourceInformation':info,'testingFeedback':['0 issues found in the first 50 simulation checks. Browser/network checks are recorded in the final validation evidence.','0 critical issues in the initial simulation suite.','Legal AI laps across all five courses and all five cars: validated real handling and ordered gates together.','A single acceleration check was narrower than the complete legal-lap matrix.'],'engineFeedback':['The full native shape registry, continuous car model, headless fixed-step simulation and controller-aware Shell fit the game well.','The documented brake-from-rest reverse behavior needed an explicit reverse:0 adaptation; native Shell owns pause and focus.','Directed route gates and one shared authoritative pursuit simulation.','Keeping native pause/input behavior aligned with live online menus and independent match clocks.','No upstream engine changes. Game adapters freeze grids, disable reversing, preserve analog input, and separate coincident cars before native contact.','Expose authoritative network timing and directed route validation as first-class racing helpers.'],'promptFeedback':['The source identity, single-file delivery names, and explicit stand-ins made the intended hand-in concrete.','The plan names ultra effort while TASK.md names max; TASK.md was used for delivery metadata.','Completing controller/touch flows and three multiplayer paths around the same simulation.','Keep the exact delivery contract separate from suggested pacing and implementation details; add a concise acceptance checklist.'],'aiHub':{'name':'Auto Motley Motor','tagline':'Little cars. Lovely roads. Big mistakes.','description':'Brake before the bend and take the scenic line through five connected county circuits. Race the clock, trade pursuit points with a friend, or find the old airfield.','type':'Game','genre':'Racing','source':'Auto Racing (1980)','series':'','players':'1–2','controls':'A/D or arrows steer; S/Down/Space brake; hold R recover; Enter confirm; Esc pause. Pad: left stick, L2 brake, Triangle recover, Options menu. Touch: drag left to steer, hold right to brake.','tags':['racing','arcade','top-down','retro','vehicles','versus']}}\npathlib.Path('../exit-interview.json').write_text(json.dumps(r,indent=2,ensure_ascii=False)+'\\\\n')\np=pathlib.Path('../PROGRESS.md');s=p.read_text().replace('A — Research and import pinned engine/Hub contracts before implementation.','C/D/E — Core/content passed; integrated UI, tour, rendering, audio and transports implemented; browser validation and repairs next.').replace('No tests yet.','50 checks passed via node tools/test.mjs sim: 25 clean legal AI laps covering every course/car, safe anchors, graph connectivity, route rejection, incident priority, brakes/analog/surfaces, storage and snapshots. Evidence in docs/evidence/ai-laps.json.')\ns+='\\\\n## Implementation resume notes\\\\n- Development sources use MM global modules. Files: data/world/sim/storage/art/audio/net/ui/main.js plus style.css.\\\\n- Pinned full engine and Hub in vendor/engine-repo and vendor/hub-repo; vendor/engine and vendor/hub symlink inside development folder.\\\\n- E.Shell is extended for themed native menus and input/focus; authoritative sim calls E.Car once/tick, reverse:0; E.Race lifecycle deliberately replaced.\\\\n- Manual/history/still research in docs/research.md. No upstream engine changes.\\\\n- Root exit-interview.json exists with preliminary factual reports, must update issue counts and final verification before completion.\\\\n- Need build pipeline and browser/Hub/direct/touch/audio acceptance next. No HTTP servers started.\\\\n'\np.write_text(s)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
